    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5c0977ae_6ed2ab3e",
        "filename": "power/apollolake.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 414,
      "author": {
        "id": 1111479
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-05-25T16:39:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Passing low always because if PMIC want to shutdown, EC should honor it ASAP.\n\nPassing high only in POWER_G3S5 (line 283), this is similar design from Skylake with SLP_SUS where EC will block the system from booting to S0 unless it has sufficient power.",
      "parentUuid": "5c0977ae_ee321bac",
      "revId": "c5b2c74e6ae37432fc14d48d08840a0ded2814fd",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5c0977ae_512666ba",
        "filename": "power/apollolake.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 414,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-05-25T17:17:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "EC does honor the value. Does it not? If the signal from the pmic is low then it\u0027ll be passed as low. \n\nIf we want to explicitly control rsmrst_l I think it should be done in less convoluted way. If rsmsrt_l is going ot be special just make it special instead of changing handle_pass_through()  to be more complicated.\n\nThe skylake code is a lot clearer since it\u0027s explicit as to what we\u0027re handling plus there are comments explaining why.",
      "parentUuid": "5c0977ae_6ed2ab3e",
      "revId": "c5b2c74e6ae37432fc14d48d08840a0ded2814fd",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
